tt-bio-demo (0.6.1) noble; urgency=medium

  * v0.6.0 was tagged and released with an empty CI gate failure: the
    version bump left debian/changelog and the onepager PDF stamped with
    the previous version, so its own build-and-install gate failed before
    the release job ever ran, and the v0.6.0 GitHub Release was published
    with zero assets. Same code, same feature (affinity questions); this
    version exists only to carry real packages. v0.6.0 is left published
    and empty rather than backfilled, matching this project's own
    standing rule that a published version's contents never change after
    the fact -- the same call made for v0.5.5.

 -- Taylor Singletary <tsingletary@tenstorrent.com>  Thu, 17 Sep 2026 16:45:00 -0700

tt-bio-demo (0.6.0) noble; urgency=medium

  * Affinity questions: the booth now answers a real question about two
    molecules together -- does this ligand bind this protein -- with
    tt-bio's nesso1 model on a chip held in reserve for it. The answer is
    the model's own score plus a highlight of the residues nearest the
    ligand, never a number alone. Off by default (opt in with
    --questions, or live with Ctrl+A); a booth left alone cycles through
    the three shipped questions on its own, or a visitor taps one from
    the gallery.
  * The quad's own fourth cell -- the one Q&A's chip reservation leaves
    empty on a 4-chip booth -- shows the question and its answer at booth
    scale, instead of sitting blank next to the small rail panel.
  * A packaged (.deb / systemd) install could enumerate zero fold
    targets, and the documented way to enable Q&A on that install path
    did not reach the daemon at all; both fixed, and verified against a
    real install on a QB2-factory-layout image, not only unit tests.
  * The `?` help card and the gallery's own caption are shorter and no
    longer claim a hardcoded chip count that Q&A's chip reservation can
    make false.
  * The docs site's molecule count and model-family count are corrected:
    "six molecules" had been stale since yeast tRNA-Phe joined the
    playlist several releases ago and was never caught, and the model
    count now matches the fourteen families the site's own runs-today
    table already listed.

 -- Taylor Singletary <tsingletary@tenstorrent.com>  Thu, 17 Sep 2026 14:00:00 -0700

tt-bio-demo (0.5.7) noble; urgency=medium

  * Pinned tt-bio moves to 0.7.3. 0.7.1/0.7.2 fixed a real attention-masking
    bug (Protenix-v2/OpenDDE padded the atom axis, not the token axis, so a
    ragged tail reached triangle attention as real unmasked key columns);
    none of this playlist's seven residue counts land on the fixed bucket
    size, so every published fold time and pLDDT was re-measured against the
    corrected path. The two shortest targets (Trp-cage, the DNA duplex) got
    slightly slower from token-bucket padding; every longer target is flat
    to slightly faster. playlist/manifest.yaml, README, the docs site and
    this handout all carry the new numbers.
  * The docs site's model-family count and commit totals are corrected for
    0.7.2's addition of Protenix-v1 (thirteen families to fourteen) and
    re-verified against the v0.7.3 tag.
  * README, INSTALL.md, the docs site and this handout are corrected to
    American English spelling throughout.

 -- Taylor Singletary <tsingletary@tenstorrent.com>  Wed, 09 Sep 2026 09:35:00 -0700

tt-bio-demo (0.5.6) noble; urgency=medium

  * Release publishing no longer refuses to fill an EMPTY release. The guard
    protecting published packages asked whether a release existed, when what
    it means to protect is the assets inside one. Creating a release in the
    GitHub UI makes the tag and the release together, so the tag push then
    found a release already there and exited before uploading anything --
    publishing v0.5.5 with no .debs at all while INSTALL.md tells operators to
    download them from exactly there. A release with assets is still refused,
    unchanged; an empty one is now filled.
  * The release job's logic moved out of the workflow YAML into
    scripts/publish-release.sh, where the test suite can drive it with a
    stubbed gh. Logic inside a workflow step can only be exercised by tagging
    a release, which is the most expensive possible place to find a bug and is
    where this one was found.
  * v0.5.5 carries the same code as this version. It is left in place with no
    packages attached rather than being back-filled, because a published
    version number that changes contents is the thing the guard exists to
    prevent -- including when the change is from nothing to something.

 -- Taylor Singletary <tsingletary@tenstorrent.com>  Mon, 31 Aug 2026 16:30:00 -0700

tt-bio-demo (0.5.5) noble; urgency=medium

  * The model weights are a checked box on every install path now. A source
    install fetched none at all -- scripts/setup-venvs.sh built both venvs and
    stopped -- so a checkout ended at a box that looked finished and could not
    fold. It fetches by default; --skip-weights opts out and a failed download
    is not fatal.
  * scripts/doctor.sh names the command that fixes it. In source mode its
    entire advice had been "they download on the first fold, or fetch them
    ahead of time", which named nothing runnable; both modes now print
    `tt-bio weights --download protenix-v2`.
  * scripts/doctor.sh no longer FAILS a booth that can fold. It required
    mols.tar at >=1GB, but tt-bio discards that archive once it is unpacked
    and `tt-bio weights --prune` deletes it; a fold loads <cache>/mols. The
    check is layered now, and asks tt-bio's own verifier when venv-runner is
    built -- the only thing that can tell a complete 1.8GB file from a
    truncated one.
  * The daemon's preflight checks the CCD molecule library. It had checked the
    checkpoint only, so a cache without the molecules reported ready and then
    died on the first fold; the list is derived from tt-bio's registry now.
  * One weights-cache resolver per language, replacing four callers that
    disagreed and none of which read $TT_BIO_CACHE. The weights postinst had
    derived the path a second time in its python half, so a host using that
    variable would have downloaded 3.7GB into a directory neither a fold nor
    the doctor would look in.
  * README and INSTALL.md document the weights step for both install paths,
    and every `tt-bio weights` command this project prints is now checked
    against the pinned tt-bio at test time.

 -- Taylor Singletary <tsingletary@tenstorrent.com>  Mon, 31 Aug 2026 09:20:00 -0700

tt-bio-demo (0.5.0) noble; urgency=medium

  * Pinned tt-bio moves to 0.7.0. Every fold time on every card, the site and
    this handout is re-measured on that release; the numbers barely move, and
    the 0.6.7 Protenix-v2 accuracy fixes are not visible on these targets
    because all seven fold without an MSA.
  * The weight-artifact API the weights postinst and the runner both call
    moved into tt_bio.weights upstream; both are ported, and the packaging
    contract test now checks the artifact key as well as the function.
  * A finished structure now holds the screen for as long as the NEXT fold can
    afford rather than a flat two seconds, capped per target by its measured
    time to first coordinates: about seven seconds ahead of the long targets,
    unchanged ahead of the short ones, whose whole collapse a flat hold would
    have suppressed.
  * The cartoon draws nucleic acids instead of dropping them. A protein/DNA
    complex used to render the protein and silently omit the DNA, and a pure
    DNA or tRNA fold fell back to the old tube renderer by way of an
    exception. Same picture as before for those two, now drawn on purpose.
  * A booth with more than one chip comes up showing all of them. --quad still
    forces the grid on a single-chip booth and the new --solo forces one large
    protein; Q toggles either way at runtime, as before.

 -- Taylor Singletary <tsingletary@tenstorrent.com>  Mon, 24 Aug 2026 11:30:00 -0700

